※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1
※ pl4g-test: run=native,qemu exit=42
※ pl4g-test: diag=none

※ A call to a pure function whose answer nothing reads is an instruction the
※ program need not run, and the optimizer drops it.  That is what makes
※ `_ ← f()` worth writing rather than merely allowed: the answer goes nowhere,
※ so with nothing else to do the call goes too.
※
※ What a dropped call takes with it is any way it had of stopping the program:
※ an overflow inside a call nobody made cannot be reached.  That follows from
※ the call being removable at all, which is what purity was declared for.

fn worked_out(n: u6) → u6:
    n + n

let seen: mut u6 = 0u6

@[impure]
fn notes(n: u6) → u6:
    ※ this one changes something, so it is made whether or not anyone looks
    seen ← n
    n

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    ※ nothing reads this, and nothing else depends on it having happened
    _ ← worked_out(3u6)
    _ ← notes(42u6)
    seen
